### Surface

A surface is a canvas that UI elements sit on. Surface colors express the visual stacking context of an app. Surface colors work together to enable sweeping changes, like dark mode.

Surface colors are reserved for the bottom layer of a surface. These elements are surfaces:

* Application background
* Panels
* Modals
* Docked containers
* Popovers

Anything that comes into the application's view and has a higher stacking context than a previous surface ultimately establishes a new surface.

<design figma-url="https://www.figma.com/design/tLYtw0hYKA22PTU3TH2r7r/?node-id=6107:67780">

![surface](https://zeroheight-uploads.s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/da925b1052c510989a8ddc?X-Amz-Algorithm=AWS4-HMAC-SHA256&X-Amz-Credential=AKIA3AVNYHQKYPFHNVW5%2F20260530%2Feu-west-1%2Fs3%2Faws4_request&X-Amz-Date=20260530T145208Z&X-Amz-Expires=604800&X-Amz-SignedHeaders=host&X-Amz-Signature=11635d405f7b4f4ada91e43a8f746f4aa75d19fc238a21ddebcd9a34c308a590)

**surface**

</design>

<callout background="2" fullWidth="true">

**ℹ️** Don't apply any decoration, brand bands, or textures to an application background.

</callout>

### Surface-container

Surface container colors are reserved for the elements that sit on top of a surface and contain other UI elements or artifacts, such as text or icons. These elements are surface containers:

* Cards or containers
* Buttons
* Button icons
* Tabsets

One of the clearest signals that something is a surface container is if it contains text or icons.

<design figma-url="https://www.figma.com/design/tLYtw0hYKA22PTU3TH2r7r/?node-id=6107:61571">

![container](https://zeroheight-uploads.s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/1d172c3c39bb8c58fac5af?X-Amz-Algorithm=AWS4-HMAC-SHA256&X-Amz-Credential=AKIA3AVNYHQKYPFHNVW5%2F20260530%2Feu-west-1%2Fs3%2Faws4_request&X-Amz-Date=20260530T145208Z&X-Amz-Expires=604800&X-Amz-SignedHeaders=host&X-Amz-Signature=c38e18218dcdaacfa26b475708f435e2dc8ae80b4787db7dbc41f0be81d4a9f7)

**container**

</design>

<callout background="2" fullWidth="true">

ℹ️ **WCAG 2.1 color contrast requirements**

To ensure proper WCAG 2.1 color contrast requirements, pair the text that sits on top of a surface with an `on` surface value. It's valid to use any of the accent or feedback colors that are used for text or icons on top of a surface color, for example, --slds-g-color-accent-1 (#066AFE).

</callout>

### On-Surface

Use the `on-surface` color value for text or icons that appear on top of a surface or surface container. This practice ensures that your content meets the color contrast requirements set by the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1.

For example, if a surface container is --slds-g-color-surface-container-3 (#E5E5E5) it will use --slds-g-color-on-surface-3 (#03234D)for the text on it.

<design figma-url="https://www.figma.com/design/tLYtw0hYKA22PTU3TH2r7r/?node-id=6107:63726">

![on-surface](https://zeroheight-uploads.s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/ed523166627e25ac451a69?X-Amz-Algorithm=AWS4-HMAC-SHA256&X-Amz-Credential=AKIA3AVNYHQKYPFHNVW5%2F20260530%2Feu-west-1%2Fs3%2Faws4_request&X-Amz-Date=20260530T145208Z&X-Amz-Expires=604800&X-Amz-SignedHeaders=host&X-Amz-Signature=725beda6afa83dcaacf4ebcaad699182935046e10ec134544eca79bb85804573)

**on-surface**

</design>

<callout background="2" fullWidth="true">

ℹ️ Text links --slds-g-color-accent-2 (#0250D9),  disabled text --slds-g-color-on-disabled-2 (#757575), and feedback text colors --slds-g-color-on-error-1 (#B60554) are available and can be found in those sections.

</callout>

### Borders

Used for borders around containers, strokes, or divider lines. There are two border colors available on light and dark backgrounds.

<design figma-url="https://www.figma.com/design/tLYtw0hYKA22PTU3TH2r7r/?node-id=6104:58464">

![border](https://zeroheight-uploads.s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/38297db4e7614b5530a1b2?X-Amz-Algorithm=AWS4-HMAC-SHA256&X-Amz-Credential=AKIA3AVNYHQKYPFHNVW5%2F20260530%2Feu-west-1%2Fs3%2Faws4_request&X-Amz-Date=20260530T145208Z&X-Amz-Expires=604800&X-Amz-SignedHeaders=host&X-Amz-Signature=e490f62cb59e4560dfcb9593bc4bb594d66a4dffbf3e5f44432d7e51f4e8838a)

**border**

</design>

<callout background="2" fullWidth="true">

#### **ℹ️ WCAG 2.1 color contrast requirements**

For compliance with WCAG 2.1 color contrast guidelines, if your component is an interactive element like a `button` or `input`, use --slds-g-color-border-2 (#5C5C5C). If your component is a non-interactive, presentational element like a divider line, use --slds-g-color-border-1 (#C9C9C9).

</callout>

